{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall have no other Elohim before Me.</b> He warns us not to accept upon ourselves as a deity any force among the supernal forces — these being the angels called Elohim — nor any of the remaining forces of the stars and constellations, and not to believe in any of them at all. And as for why He called them 'others' (אחרים), they said in the Sifri: because they are 'other' to their worshippers. Another interpretation: 'others' — today of gold, tomorrow of silver, tomorrow of wood. And the fundamental reason is that they are called 'others' because they receive power from another (מאחר), and have no power of their own. And this is the meaning of ״לא תשתחוה לאל אחר״ — to an El that receives from another — but to the Holy One, blessed be He, alone, who is the Elohim of Israel, who does not receive power from another. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted ״ומבלעדי אין אלהים״ — 'for I have not received My sovereignty from another.'" "<b>Before Me.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'before Myself,' in the manner of (II Samuel 17:11) ״ופניך הולכים בקרב״. Or the sense of על פני may be 'in any place I face,' and this is a warning against [worshipping] any place in the world, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is in every place." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the secret of the phrase על פני is 'before My face,' as it is written (Deuteronomy 5:4) ״פנים בפנים דבר ה׳ עמכם״. And the secret in the word אחרים is that He did not say לא יהיה לך אל אחר על פני but rather אחרים, in the manner of (Genesis 31:50) ״ואם תקח נשים על בנותי״ — for it would not be possible to say ״אם תקח אשה על בנותי״; and I have already explained this in the verse וידבר.<br><b>Ramban wrote</b> that the concept of idolatry falls into three categories: there are those who worship angels, there are those who worship the stars and constellations, the entire host of heaven, and there are those who worship demons. At first the ancients began with the worship of angels, for they knew that they possessed the power of dominion over the nations, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 10:13) ״שר יון״ and ״שר מלכות פרס״, and each one would worship his own prince who was appointed over him; and this is what is written concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, (Deuteronomy 10:17) ״הוא אלהי האלהים״, for the angels are called Elohim, and the Holy One, blessed be He, is Elohim over them, and likewise (Psalms 97:7) ״השתחוו לו כל אלהים״, and Jethro said (Exodus 18:11) ״כי גדול ה׳ מכל האלהים״, and likewise our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'they called Him אלהא דאלהיא — the El of elohim.' After that they turned to the worship of the stars and constellations, the entire host of heaven, for they knew they possessed great power and ability over lower things, and this is what is written (Jeremiah 8:2) ״ושטחום לשמש ולירח ולכל צבא השמים אשר אהבום ואשר עבדום ואשר הלכו אחריהם ואשר דרשום ואשר השתחוו להם״, for they were great sages knowing the power of the constellation according to its governance over their land, and they thought that through the worship of those forces their constellation would prevail and benefit them. And after that they turned to worshipping demons, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 32:17) ״אלהים לא ידעום חדשים מקרוב באו״ — Scripture mocks them, that they sacrificed even to demons who are not deity at all, as it is said (ibid.) ״יזבחו לשדים לא אלוה״, who are not like the angels called אלוה, but they are 'Elohim that their fathers knew not,' meaning that they found in them no divinity or sovereignty at all, and they are 'new' to them, for they learned to do so only recently from the sorcerer Egyptians. And therefore He warns: לא יהיה לך אלהים אחרים — these being the first category, and this is the meaning of על פני as I alluded to its secret. End of quote."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:32.199208867Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}